const cases = [
["msk-w11-08","Gout","gouty arthritis","A patient presents with an acutely painful swollen joint.|The pain reached severe intensity within hours.|Even the touch of a bedsheet is painful.|The first metatarsophalangeal joint is involved.|Serum urate is normal during the attack.|Joint aspirate shows needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals.","The abrupt first MTP flare fits gout, and the crystal shape and birefringence identify monosodium urate. Serum urate can be normal during a flare."],
["cardio-w2-03","Acute pericarditis","pericarditis","A patient has sharp chest pain.|It worsens with inspiration.|Lying flat makes it worse, while leaning forward helps.|A friction rub is audible.|Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion.|ECG shows widespread ST elevation with PR depression.","Pleuritic chest pain, a friction rub, typical ECG changes and an effusion are the four features in the lecture’s diagnostic framework. Two are sufficient."],
["endo-w2-03","Graves disease","graves;graves' disease","A patient loses weight despite an increased appetite.|They report heat intolerance and increased sweating.|Examination shows a fine tremor and tachycardia.|The thyroid is symmetrically enlarged.|There is proptosis with periorbital swelling.|Radioactive iodine uptake is diffusely increased.","Symmetric thyroid enlargement, orbitopathy and diffuse uptake distinguish Graves disease from nodular causes and thyroiditis."],
["neuro-w14-06","Cluster headache","cluster;cluster headaches","A patient has recurrent attacks of severe headache.|The pain stays strictly on one side around the eye.|During attacks the patient paces restlessly.|Episodes last 15 to 180 minutes.|Several episodes can occur in a single day.|The eye waters and the nose becomes congested on the painful side.","Short, severe orbital attacks with restlessness and ipsilateral autonomic symptoms fit cluster headache."],
["gi-w11-08","Crohn disease","crohn;crohn's disease;crohns disease","A patient has chronic diarrhea and weight loss.|Abdominal pain is mainly in the right lower quadrant.|Oral ulcers are present.|Endoscopy shows involved segments separated by healthy mucosa.|The mucosa has a cobblestone appearance.|Inflammation is transmural, with a fistula.","Skip lesions and transmural inflammation with fistula formation support Crohn disease."],
["msk-w11-05","Psoriatic arthritis","psa","A patient develops inflammatory joint pain.|Joint involvement is asymmetric.|Distal interphalangeal joints are affected.|One entire digit is swollen.|There is nail pitting and lifting of the nail plate.|Exam finds psoriasis in a hidden site, the scalp.","Asymmetric DIP arthritis, dactylitis and nail changes point to psoriatic arthritis. The notes recommend looking for psoriasis in hidden sites."],
["cardio-w2-04","Aortic stenosis","as","An older adult presents with dyspnea.|They also report angina and syncope.|There is a sustained left ventricular heave.|The carotid pulse is small and slow to rise.|A systolic ejection murmur is heard at the base.|The murmur radiates to the carotids.","A basal ejection murmur radiating to the carotids, with a delayed low-amplitude pulse, supports aortic stenosis."],
["endo-w3-01","Primary adrenal insufficiency","addison disease;addison's disease;addisons disease;addison","A patient presents with weakness and weight loss.|They report nausea and abdominal discomfort.|Standing brings on presyncope.|Sodium is low and potassium is elevated.|There is skin hyperpigmentation.|Morning cortisol is low and ACTH is elevated.","Elevated ACTH with low cortisol localizes the problem to the adrenal gland. Aldosterone loss accounts for the electrolyte pattern."],
["neuro-w15-03","Parkinson disease","parkinson;parkinson's disease;parkinsons disease;pd","A patient notices a gradually worsening hand tremor.|It is more prominent on one side.|Handwriting has become smaller.|Movements are slow and difficult to initiate.|Examination shows rigidity that does not depend on movement speed.|The tremor is most evident at rest.","Asymmetric resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity and micrographia fit Parkinson disease."],
["gi-w11-08","Ulcerative colitis","uc","A patient reports fatigue and abdominal pain around bowel movements.|Diarrhea wakes them at night.|There is visible blood in the stool.|Colonoscopy shows friable mucosa and pseudopolyps.|Colonic involvement is continuous.|Inflammation is limited to the mucosa and submucosa.","Bloody diarrhea with continuous, superficial colonic inflammation fits ulcerative colitis."],
["msk-w11-05","Ankylosing spondylitis","as;radiographic axial spondyloarthritis;r-axspa","Back pain began before age 40.|Symptoms crept in over several months.|Morning stiffness lasts over an hour.|The pain wakes the patient at night and improves with exercise.|Imaging shows bilateral, symmetric sacroiliitis.|New bone formation has produced a bamboo-spine appearance.","The pain pattern is inflammatory. Symmetric sacroiliitis and spinal fusion support ankylosing spondylitis."],
["resp-w6-06","Asthma","","A patient has episodes of breathlessness.|They have a family history of atopic disease.|Episodes can follow a viral upper respiratory infection.|Allergen exposure also triggers symptoms.|Spirometry shows airflow obstruction.|FEV₁ increases by at least 12% after a bronchodilator.","Variable symptoms and reversible airflow obstruction fit the asthma pattern described in the lecture."],
["cardio-w5-05","Atrial fibrillation","af;afib;a fib","An older adult reports palpitations and dyspnea.|The ventricular rate is rapid.|Loss of atrial contraction can impair ventricular filling.|The rhythm carries a risk of atrial thromboembolism.|R–R intervals vary without a repeating pattern.|The ECG shows disorganized atrial activity with fibrillatory waves.","The irregularly irregular ventricular rhythm and fibrillatory activity identify atrial fibrillation."],
["msk-w11-07","Giant cell arteritis","gca;temporal arteritis","A patient over 50 develops a new headache.|They report fatigue and weight loss.|The scalp is tender.|Chewing brings on jaw pain.|ESR and CRP are elevated.|The temporal artery is tender and feels thickened and rope-like.","New headache after age 50, scalp tenderness and jaw claudication fit giant cell arteritis, which often involves the temporal arteries."],
["neuro-w14-02","Multiple sclerosis","ms","A patient presents with vision loss.|Eye movement is painful.|A separate episode involved sensory changes and weakness.|MRI shows lesions in the periventricular region and spinal cord.|A follow-up MRI shows a new lesion.|CSF contains oligoclonal bands.","Optic neuritis and myelitis are typical presentations. The lesion locations and a new lesion on follow-up demonstrate dissemination in space and time."],
["msk-w11-08","CPP crystal arthritis","pseudogout;cppd;calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease;calcium pyrophosphate crystal arthritis","An older adult presents with acute monoarthritis.|They have underlying osteoarthritis.|The knee is involved.|Pain took longer to peak than a typical gout flare.|Joint aspirate contains rhomboid crystals.|The crystals are positively birefringent.","Rhomboid, positively birefringent calcium pyrophosphate crystals identify CPP crystal arthritis, often called pseudogout."],
["msk-w11-05","Reactive arthritis","rea","A young adult develops pain in a few joints.|The affected joints are large.|Enthesitis accompanies the joint symptoms.|The symptoms started one to three weeks after a gastrointestinal infection.|Eye and urinary symptoms accompany the arthritis.|The joint itself is not infected.","The combination of oligoarthritis, enthesitis and a recent extra-articular infection supports reactive arthritis."],
["msk-w11-04","Osteoarthritis","oa","A patient reports knee pain on most days.|Symptoms have lasted longer than six weeks.|Activity worsens the aching.|Morning stiffness lasts less than 30 minutes.|There is a cool joint effusion.|Examination shows joint thickening and crepitus.","Chronic activity-related pain, brief morning stiffness and crepitus support osteoarthritis."],
["cardio-w2-06","Mitral stenosis","ms","A patient with previous rheumatic fever develops valvular disease.|The left atrium enlarges.|Atrial fibrillation develops.|The resulting atrial thrombus risk can lead to stroke.|An opening snap is audible.|A mid-diastolic rumble is best heard at the apex in the left lateral position.","An opening snap and apical mid-diastolic rumble are typical of rheumatic mitral stenosis."],
["cardio-w2-03","Cardiac tamponade","tamponade;pericardial tamponade","A patient has progressive breathlessness that now occurs at rest.|They feel light-headed walking.|Blood pressure is low and the jugular venous pressure is elevated.|Heart sounds are muffled.|ECG shows electrical alternans.|Echo shows a large effusion and a swinging heart with impaired filling.","Hypotension, elevated JVP and muffled heart sounds form Beck’s triad. The effusion compromises cardiac filling."],
["neuro-w15-03","Essential tremor","et","A patient presents with a gradually worsening tremor.|It affects both sides symmetrically.|The head and voice are also involved.|Handwriting is large and shaky.|The tremor occurs with posture or action.|Alcohol temporarily improves the tremor.","Symmetric action or postural tremor, head and voice involvement and improvement with alcohol support essential tremor."],
["endo-w2-03","Subacute thyroiditis","de quervain thyroiditis;de quervain's thyroiditis","A patient develops symptoms of thyrotoxicosis.|They recently had an upper respiratory tract infection.|The neck is painful and tender.|Swallowing is painful.|Radioactive iodine uptake is low.|The illness may be followed by a hypothyroid phase.","The recent infection and painful thyroid point to subacute thyroiditis. Low uptake fits release of stored hormone from the inflamed gland."],
["resp-w6-06","Cystic fibrosis","cf","A patient is investigated for a chronic obstructive lung condition.|The condition follows an autosomal recessive pattern.|The implicated gene is on chromosome 7.|Sweat chloride is elevated.|A repeat sweat test and mutation analysis support the diagnosis.|Testing identifies a CFTR mutation.","Elevated sweat chloride and a disease-causing CFTR mutation fit the cystic fibrosis pattern in the obstructive lung disease notes."],
["neuro-w14-06","Migraine with aura","migraine;migraine with visual aura","A patient has episodic headaches lasting several hours.|The pain is pulsating and worsens with routine activity.|They experience nausea and sensitivity to light and sound.|Some attacks are preceded by visual zigzags.|The visual symptoms spread gradually and fully resolve.|The visual phase lasts between 5 and 60 minutes.","The headache features fit migraine, and the gradual, fully reversible visual symptoms fit an aura."],
["b3-w9-03","Iron deficiency anemia","IDA;iron deficiency","A patient reports fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance.|They have progressively heavier menstrual bleeding.|They have developed a craving to chew ice.|The CBC shows microcytosis and an elevated red cell distribution width.|Transferrin is elevated and transferrin saturation is low.|Serum ferritin is markedly low.","Low ferritin indicates depleted iron stores. Low transferrin saturation and elevated transferrin support iron deficiency in the setting of chronic blood loss."],
["b3-w9-05","Vitamin B12 deficiency","B12 deficiency;cobalamin deficiency","An older adult presents with fatigue and trouble walking in the dark.|They have tingling in both feet.|Vibration and position sense are reduced at the toes.|The CBC shows macrocytic anemia.|The smear shows macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils.|Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are both elevated.","Neurologic findings and elevated methylmalonic acid point to vitamin B12 deficiency. Folate deficiency can also cause megaloblastic anemia and elevated homocysteine."],
["b3-w9-06","Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia","warm AIHA;warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","A patient develops fatigue and jaundice over several days.|The reticulocyte count is elevated.|Indirect bilirubin and LDH are high, and haptoglobin is low.|The smear shows spherocytes.|The direct antiglobulin test is positive.|Red cells are coated with IgG.","Spherocytes and biochemical evidence of hemolysis narrow the differential. IgG detected on the direct antiglobulin test supports warm autoimmune hemolysis."],
["b3-w9-06","G6PD deficiency","glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","A patient develops dark urine and fatigue over two days.|They recently started an oxidant medication.|Indirect bilirubin is elevated and haptoglobin is low.|The direct antiglobulin test is negative.|The smear shows bite cells.|A supravital stain shows Heinz bodies.","Oxidative stress causes hemoglobin to precipitate as Heinz bodies. Splenic removal of these inclusions produces bite cells."],
["b3-w10-05","Immune thrombocytopenia","ITP;immune thrombocytopenic purpura","A child presents with new bruising.|They recovered from a viral illness two weeks ago.|Examination shows petechiae and the child otherwise looks well.|Platelets are markedly reduced, with normal hemoglobin and white cell counts.|PT and aPTT are normal.|The smear shows some large platelets and no blasts or schistocytes.","Isolated thrombocytopenia in a well child after a viral illness supports immune thrombocytopenia after other causes are excluded."],
["b3-w10-05","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","TTP","An adult presents with fatigue and new bruising.|They have intermittent confusion.|The CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.|The blood film shows schistocytes.|PT and aPTT are normal.|ADAMTS13 activity is below 10%.","Microangiopathic hemolysis and thrombocytopenia suggest a thrombotic microangiopathy. Severe ADAMTS13 deficiency identifies TTP."],
["b3-w11-04","Acute promyelocytic leukemia","APL;acute promyelocytic leukaemia","A young adult presents with fatigue and bleeding gums.|The CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.|Coagulation testing shows low fibrinogen and elevated D-dimer.|The smear shows abnormal promyelocytes.|Some cells contain bundles of Auer rods.|Testing detects a PML::RARA fusion from t(15;17).","The abnormal promyelocytes and coagulopathy suggest acute promyelocytic leukemia. The PML::RARA fusion identifies the subtype."],
["b3-w11-04","Chronic myeloid leukemia","CML;chronic myelogenous leukemia;chronic myeloid leukaemia","An adult reports fatigue and early satiety.|Examination shows splenomegaly.|The CBC shows marked leukocytosis.|The differential shows granulocytes at several stages of maturation.|Basophils are increased.|Testing detects BCR::ABL1 from t(9;22).","The granulocytic left shift and basophilia suggest CML. BCR::ABL1 confirms the diagnosis in this clinical setting."],
["b3-w11-04","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","CLL;chronic lymphocytic leukaemia","An older adult has an abnormal CBC on routine testing.|They feel well and have no constitutional symptoms.|Repeat testing shows persistent absolute lymphocytosis.|The smear shows small mature lymphocytes and smudge cells.|Flow cytometry shows a clonal B-cell population expressing CD5 and CD23.|The circulating clonal B-cell count has stayed above 5 × 10⁹/L for more than three months.","A persistent clonal B-cell count above the diagnostic threshold and a typical immunophenotype support CLL."],
["b3-w11-08","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","ALL;B-ALL;B cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia;acute lymphoblastic leukaemia","A school-age child presents with fatigue and fever.|They have leg pain and easy bruising.|The CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.|The blood film shows blasts.|Bone marrow is extensively infiltrated by immature cells expressing TdT.|Flow cytometry shows that the blasts express B-cell markers including CD19.","Bone marrow replacement explains the cytopenias and bone pain. TdT with B-lineage markers identifies precursor B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia."],
["b3-w11-05","Polycythemia vera","PV;polycythaemia vera","An adult reports headaches and episodic dizziness.|They describe itching after a hot shower.|Hemoglobin and hematocrit are persistently elevated.|Platelets are also elevated.|Serum erythropoietin is low.|Testing detects JAK2 V617F.","Persistent erythrocytosis with low erythropoietin and a JAK2 mutation strongly supports polycythemia vera."],
["b3-w12-04","Classical Hodgkin lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma;Hodgkin disease;cHL","A young adult notices a painless lump in the neck.|They report drenching night sweats.|The nodes have enlarged over several weeks.|Chest imaging shows a mediastinal mass.|Node biopsy shows large binucleate cells in a mixed inflammatory background.|The atypical cells are positive for CD30 and CD15.","Reed–Sternberg cells in an inflammatory background with this immunophenotype support classical Hodgkin lymphoma."],
["b3-w12-06","Multiple myeloma","myeloma;plasma cell myeloma","An older adult presents with persistent back pain and fatigue.|Bloodwork shows anemia and declining renal function.|Serum calcium is elevated.|Imaging shows several lytic bone lesions.|Serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal spike.|Bone marrow shows 25% clonal plasma cells.","Clonal marrow plasma cells with attributable end-organ findings such as lytic lesions establish active multiple myeloma."],
["b1-w3-07","Turner syndrome","monosomy X;45,X","A teenager is evaluated for primary amenorrhea.|They have been short throughout childhood.|Breast development is limited.|Examination shows a webbed neck.|They have a history of aortic coarctation.|Karyotyping shows 45,X.","Short stature, ovarian insufficiency, and coarctation fit Turner syndrome. The karyotype confirms monosomy X in this case."],
["b2-w7-07","Delirium","acute confusional state","An older inpatient starts acting differently after admission.|Family members say the change began yesterday.|The patient is drowsy in the morning and restless that evening.|They have trouble following a conversation.|They cannot sustain attention long enough to recite the months backward.|Their attention and alertness fluctuate on repeated assessments.","An acute, fluctuating change in attention and alertness is characteristic of delirium."],
["b4-w13-15","Clostridioides difficile infection","C difficile;C diff;CDI;pseudomembranous colitis;Clostridium difficile infection","A hospitalized patient develops new watery diarrhea.|They also have lower abdominal cramping.|They recently finished a broad-spectrum antibiotic course.|They have fever and leukocytosis.|Endoscopy performed for diagnostic uncertainty shows adherent yellow-white plaques.|Testing detects C. difficile toxin in an unformed stool sample.","The recent antibiotic exposure, symptomatic diarrhea, and stool toxin support C. difficile infection. The plaques are pseudomembranes."],
["b3-w9-03","Beta thalassemia trait","beta thalassemia minor;beta-thalassaemia trait","A young adult has incidental anemia.|They feel well and report no bleeding.|The MCV is markedly low.|The red cell count is relatively high for the hemoglobin level.|Ferritin is normal and the smear shows target cells.|Hemoglobin analysis shows elevated HbA2.","Microcytosis out of proportion to the anemia and elevated HbA2 support beta thalassemia trait."],
["b3-w9-03","Anemia of chronic inflammation","anemia of chronic disease;Anemia of inflammation","A patient with longstanding inflammatory arthritis reports fatigue.|Anemia develops during several months of active disease.|The red cells are mildly microcytic.|Serum iron is low.|Transferrin is low.|Ferritin is elevated despite low transferrin saturation.","Inflammation raises hepcidin, which limits available iron. Low transferrin and preserved or elevated ferritin favour inflammation over isolated iron deficiency."],
["b3-w9-05","Folate deficiency","folic acid deficiency;vitamin B9 deficiency","A patient presents with fatigue and a sore tongue.|They have had markedly limited oral intake for months.|Vibration and position sense are intact.|The smear shows macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils.|Homocysteine is elevated but methylmalonic acid is normal.|Serum folate is low.","Megaloblastic changes and high homocysteine fit either folate or B12 deficiency. Normal methylmalonic acid and low folate support folate deficiency here."],
["b3-w9-06","Hereditary spherocytosis","HS","An adolescent has intermittent jaundice.|A parent had a splenectomy for chronic anemia.|The spleen is enlarged.|The reticulocyte count is elevated.|The smear shows spherocytes and the direct antiglobulin test is negative.|Eosin-5-maleimide binding is reduced.","Inherited red cell membrane defects produce spherocytes and splenic hemolysis. Reduced eosin-5-maleimide binding supports the diagnosis."],
["b3-w9-06","Sickle cell disease","sickle cell anemia;HbSS","A child presents with recurrent episodes of severe limb pain.|There is a history of painful swelling of the hands in infancy.|The child has chronic anemia with reticulocytosis.|The spleen is small on ultrasound.|The smear shows elongated, curved red cells.|Hemoglobin analysis shows predominantly HbS and no HbA before transfusion.","HbS polymerization causes sickling, hemolysis, and vaso-occlusion. The hemoglobin pattern here is consistent with HbSS."],
["b3-w9-06","Cold agglutinin disease","cold autoimmune hemolytic anemia;cold agglutinin haemolytic anaemia","An older adult reports fatigue and episodic dark urine.|Their fingers turn dusky in the cold.|Laboratory testing supports hemolysis.|Red cell clumps disperse when the sample is warmed.|The direct antiglobulin test detects C3d but no IgG.|A clinically significant cold agglutinin titre is found without an associated infection.","Cold-reactive antibodies activate complement. Cold-related symptoms, red cell agglutination, and a C3d-positive direct antiglobulin test fit this diagnosis."],
["b3-w9-08","Myelodysplastic syndrome","MDS;myelodysplastic neoplasm","An older adult reports fatigue and multiple recent infections.|The CBC shows macrocytic anemia and neutropenia.|Vitamin B12 and folate levels are normal.|The smear shows hypogranular neutrophils with hyposegmented nuclei.|Bone marrow shows dysplasia in multiple cell lines.|Cytogenetic testing shows an acquired clonal abnormality.","Persistent cytopenias with marrow dysplasia and evidence of clonality support a myelodysplastic neoplasm."],
["b3-w10-03","Von Willebrand disease","vWD;von Willebrand's disease","A young adult has longstanding easy bruising.|They report recurrent nosebleeds and heavy menstrual bleeding.|A parent had prolonged bleeding after dental work.|The platelet count and PT are normal.|Von Willebrand factor antigen is reduced.|Von Willebrand factor platelet-binding activity is also reduced.","Reduced von Willebrand factor impairs platelet adhesion and often causes mucocutaneous bleeding."],
["b3-w10-04","Hemophilia A","haemophilia A;factor VIII deficiency","A boy presents with a swollen knee after minor trauma.|Joint aspiration shows blood.|A maternal uncle had recurrent joint bleeds.|PT is normal and aPTT is prolonged.|The aPTT corrects with a mixing study.|Factor VIII activity is very low and factor IX activity is normal.","Deep bleeding with low factor VIII activity fits hemophilia A. Correction on mixing supports deficiency of a coagulation factor."],
["b3-w10-03","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","DIC","A critically ill patient begins bleeding from venipuncture sites.|They were admitted with septic shock.|The platelet count is falling on serial CBCs.|PT and aPTT are prolonged.|Fibrinogen is falling.|D-dimer is markedly elevated.","Systemic activation of coagulation consumes platelets and coagulation factors. Falling fibrinogen and rising fibrin breakdown products support DIC in this setting."],
["b3-w10-05","Shiga toxin-associated hemolytic uremic syndrome","STEC HUS;typical HUS;hemolytic uremic syndrome","A child develops pallor and reduced urine output.|They had abdominal cramps and bloody diarrhea several days earlier.|Creatinine is elevated.|Platelets are low and the smear shows schistocytes.|ADAMTS13 activity is not severely reduced.|Stool testing detects Shiga toxin.","Bloody diarrhea followed by microangiopathic hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury fits Shiga toxin-associated HUS."],
["b3-w10-05","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","HIT;HIT type 2","A postoperative patient develops unilateral leg swelling.|They have received unfractionated heparin since surgery.|The platelet count falls by more than half on day six.|Ultrasound shows a new proximal deep vein thrombosis.|An immunoassay detects antibodies against platelet factor 4–heparin complexes.|A serotonin release assay is positive.","The timing and new thrombosis are characteristic. The functional assay shows the antibodies activate platelets."],
["b3-w11-06","Essential thrombocythemia","ET;essential thrombocythaemia","An adult has persistently elevated platelets on routine testing.|They describe intermittent burning discomfort in their toes.|Iron studies and inflammatory markers are normal.|There is no sustained erythrocytosis.|Marrow shows proliferation of large, mature megakaryocytes with hyperlobulated nuclei.|Testing detects a CALR mutation.","Persistent thrombocytosis with characteristic marrow morphology and a myeloproliferative driver mutation supports essential thrombocythemia."],
["b3-w11-04","Acute myeloid leukemia","AML;acute myeloid leukaemia","An older adult has fatigue and recurrent infections.|There are new bruises on the arms.|The CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.|The blood film shows circulating blasts.|Marrow contains 35% blasts expressing myeloperoxidase.|Auer rods are present and PML::RARA testing is negative.","Myeloperoxidase and Auer rods identify myeloid differentiation. The blast burden supports acute myeloid leukemia."],
["b3-w12-04","Burkitt lymphoma","Burkitt's lymphoma","A child develops rapidly progressive abdominal distension.|Imaging shows a bulky abdominal mass.|LDH is markedly elevated.|Biopsy shows a dense population of medium-sized lymphoid cells.|Tingible-body macrophages produce a starry-sky pattern and Ki-67 approaches 100%.|Testing shows a MYC::IGH rearrangement.","Rapid growth, the biopsy appearance, and a MYC rearrangement support Burkitt lymphoma."],
["b3-w12-04","Follicular lymphoma","FL","An older adult has painless lymph nodes that wax and wane.|They have no fever or weight loss.|Imaging shows lymphadenopathy in several regions.|Biopsy shows crowded neoplastic follicles.|The B cells express CD10 and BCL2.|Testing shows an IGH::BCL2 rearrangement.","Indolent lymphadenopathy and BCL2-positive germinal centre follicles with an IGH::BCL2 rearrangement support follicular lymphoma."],
["b3-w12-04","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","DLBCL","An older adult notices an enlarging neck mass.|The mass doubles in size over several weeks.|They have drenching night sweats.|Biopsy shows loss of normal nodal architecture.|Large atypical lymphoid cells grow in diffuse sheets.|The tumour cells express B-cell markers including CD20 and PAX5.","An aggressive clinical course and a diffuse infiltrate of large B cells fit diffuse large B-cell lymphoma."],
["b3-w12-06","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","MGUS","An older adult is referred for an incidental serum protein abnormality.|They feel well and report no bone pain.|Hemoglobin, calcium, and kidney function are normal.|Imaging shows no lytic lesions.|The serum IgG monoclonal protein measures 12 g/L.|Bone marrow shows 4% clonal plasma cells.","A small monoclonal protein and fewer than 10% clonal marrow plasma cells, without attributable end-organ damage, fit MGUS."],
["b3-w12-06","Smoldering multiple myeloma","SMM;smouldering multiple myeloma","A patient is referred after an abnormal serum protein electrophoresis.|They report no bone pain or recurrent infections.|Hemoglobin, calcium, and kidney function are normal.|The monoclonal protein measures 34 g/L.|Bone marrow contains 20% clonal plasma cells.|There are no myeloma-defining events on laboratory testing or imaging.","The clonal burden exceeds the MGUS range. In the absence of myeloma-defining events, the findings fit smoldering myeloma."],
["b1-w3-07","Klinefelter syndrome","47,XXY;Klinefelter's syndrome","A young adult is evaluated for infertility.|They are tall with relatively long limbs.|Examination shows small firm testes.|There is mild gynecomastia.|Testosterone is low with elevated LH and FSH.|Karyotyping shows 47,XXY.","Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism and the 47,XXY karyotype support Klinefelter syndrome."],
["b1-w3-07","22q11.2 deletion syndrome","DiGeorge syndrome;velocardiofacial syndrome","An infant has poor feeding and recurrent infections.|The infant has a conotruncal heart defect.|Calcium is low in the setting of low parathyroid hormone.|The thymic shadow is small.|Testing shows reduced T-cell numbers.|Chromosomal microarray shows a deletion at 22q11.2.","Conotruncal disease, hypoparathyroidism, and thymic hypoplasia fit a 22q11.2 deletion."],
["b2-w6-04","Fibroadenoma","breast fibroadenoma","A young adult notices a painless breast lump.|There is no nipple discharge or skin change.|The lump is firm, smooth, and mobile.|Ultrasound shows a circumscribed oval solid mass.|It is wider than tall on ultrasound.|Core biopsy shows benign epithelial and stromal proliferation.","A circumscribed mobile mass with benign biphasic histology supports fibroadenoma."],
["b4-w15-05","Common variable immunodeficiency","CVID","A young adult has recurrent bacterial sinus and lung infections.|They had an initially unremarkable childhood.|Serum IgG and IgA are persistently low.|They mount a poor antibody response to vaccination.|Secondary causes of hypogammaglobulinemia are excluded.|Circulating B cells are present, but antibody production is impaired.","Low immunoglobulins, poor functional antibody responses, and exclusion of secondary causes support common variable immunodeficiency."],
["cardio-w2-05","Aortic regurgitation","AR;aortic insufficiency","An adult notices exertional breathlessness.|They report prominent heartbeats when lying down.|Blood pressure shows a wide pulse pressure.|Peripheral pulses are bounding.|A high-pitched early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard along the left sternal border.|Doppler shows diastolic flow from the aorta into the left ventricle.","Backward flow into the left ventricle during diastole explains the murmur and widened pulse pressure."],
["cardio-w2-07","Mitral regurgitation","MR;mitral insufficiency","An adult reports increasing exertional dyspnea.|They have occasional palpitations.|The apex beat is displaced laterally.|There is a blowing holosystolic murmur at the apex.|The murmur radiates toward the axilla.|Doppler shows systolic flow from the left ventricle into the left atrium.","Systolic backflow into the left atrium produces the apical holosystolic murmur."],
["cardio-w2-09","Infective endocarditis","endocarditis","An adult has two weeks of fever and fatigue.|They have a new regurgitant murmur.|Physical examination shows splinter hemorrhages.|Imaging shows a splenic infarct.|Separate blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus.|Echocardiography shows a valvular vegetation.","Sustained typical bacteremia and a valvular vegetation support infective endocarditis."],
["cardio-w3-04","Inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction","inferior STEMI;STEMI;inferior myocardial infarction","A patient develops persistent chest pressure at rest.|They are nauseated and diaphoretic.|The discomfort radiates into the left arm.|ECG shows reciprocal ST depression in I and aVL.|There is ST elevation in II, III, and aVF.|Angiography shows an acute right coronary artery occlusion.","The inferior-lead ST elevations with reciprocal changes localize the infarct to the inferior wall."],
["cardio-w5-04","Atrial flutter","AFL","A patient reports several hours of palpitations.|Their pulse is regular at approximately 150 per minute.|ECG shows a narrow QRS complex.|Atrial activity is approximately 300 per minute.|Only every second atrial impulse conducts to the ventricles.|Inferior leads show regular sawtooth flutter waves.","An atrial rate near 300 with typical flutter waves and 2:1 conduction accounts for the regular ventricular rate near 150."],
["resp-w6-06","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","COPD","An older adult has slowly progressive exertional breathlessness.|They have a longstanding productive cough.|There is a substantial cigarette smoking history.|Examination shows prolonged expiration.|Spirometry shows persistent airflow obstruction.|The post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC remains below 0.70.","Persistent post-bronchodilator airflow obstruction in this exposure and symptom context supports COPD."],
["resp-w7-07","Primary spontaneous pneumothorax","pneumothorax;PSP","A tall young adult develops abrupt unilateral chest pain.|They have sudden breathlessness at rest.|They report no trauma or known lung disease.|Breath sounds are reduced on the painful side.|The patient is hemodynamically stable.|Chest radiography shows a visceral pleural line with no lung markings beyond it.","Air in the pleural space explains the radiograph and abrupt symptoms. The absence of trauma or known lung disease supports a primary spontaneous event."],
["resp-w8-02","Community-acquired pneumonia","pneumonia;CAP","An adult presents with abrupt fever and malaise.|They have a new productive cough.|The chest hurts when they take a deep breath.|There is focal bronchial breathing.|Chest radiography shows a new lobar airspace opacity.|They had no recent hospitalization before the onset of symptoms.","An acute febrile respiratory illness with a new focal infiltrate supports community-acquired pneumonia."],
["ent-w9-07","Croup","laryngotracheitis;viral croup","A toddler develops rhinorrhea and a low fever.|A cough becomes much worse at night.|The cough has a barking quality.|The voice is hoarse.|Inspiratory stridor becomes more audible when the child cries.|There is no drooling and the child swallows fluids.","Barking cough, hoarseness, and inspiratory stridor after a viral prodrome point to croup."],
["ent-w9-06","Thyroglossal duct cyst","TGDC","A school-age child has a painless lump in the front of the neck.|It became more noticeable after an upper respiratory infection.|The lump is in the midline.|It elevates during swallowing.|It also rises when the child protrudes the tongue.|Ultrasound shows a cyst near the hyoid and a separate normally located thyroid.","Movement with tongue protrusion and a midline cyst near the hyoid are characteristic of a thyroglossal duct cyst."],
["gi-w10-04","Achalasia","esophageal achalasia;oesophageal achalasia","An adult reports progressively difficult swallowing.|Both liquids and solids have been difficult to swallow from early on.|They regurgitate undigested food at night.|Endoscopy finds no obstructing lesion.|A barium study shows distal tapering with proximal dilation.|Manometry shows absent peristalsis and impaired relaxation of the esophagogastric junction.","Loss of peristalsis and impaired junction relaxation on manometry support achalasia."],
["gi-w11-03","Celiac disease","coeliac disease","An adult reports chronic bloating and loose stools.|They have otherwise unexplained iron deficiency.|There has been unintentional weight loss.|Total IgA is normal.|IgA tissue transglutaminase antibodies are elevated on a gluten-containing diet.|Duodenal biopsies show villous atrophy and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes.","Serology and characteristic small-bowel histology in a patient eating gluten support celiac disease."],
["gi-w12-04","Acute cholecystitis","cholecystitis","An adult develops right upper quadrant pain after eating.|The pain persists through the night.|They develop fever and leukocytosis.|Palpation during inspiration causes abrupt inspiratory arrest.|Ultrasound shows a stone lodged near the gallbladder neck.|There is gallbladder wall thickening and pericholecystic fluid.","Sustained cystic duct obstruction with local inflammation explains the persistent pain and ultrasound findings."],
["gi-w12-04","Acute cholangitis","ascending cholangitis;bacterial cholangitis","An adult presents with fever and chills.|They have right upper quadrant pain.|The sclerae are icteric.|The liver panel shows a cholestatic pattern.|Imaging shows a dilated common bile duct with a distal stone.|Drainage yields purulent bile.","Fever and jaundice in the setting of obstructed bile flow suggest infection of the biliary tree."],
["gi-w12-09","Acute pancreatitis","pancreatitis","An adult presents with severe upper abdominal pain.|The pain radiates through to the back.|They have persistent vomiting.|The epigastrium is tender.|Serum lipase is greater than three times the upper limit of normal.|Imaging shows pancreatic edema and peripancreatic inflammation.","Characteristic pain and a marked lipase elevation support acute pancreatitis."],
["gi-w13-07","Acute appendicitis","appendicitis","An adult presents with worsening abdominal pain.|It started around the umbilicus.|They have anorexia and low-grade fever.|The pain migrates to the right lower quadrant.|There is localized guarding and tenderness.|Imaging shows a dilated inflamed blind-ending structure arising from the cecum.","Migratory pain and imaging showing an inflamed appendix support acute appendicitis."],
["gu-w16-05","Testicular torsion","torsion of the testis","An adolescent develops sudden severe unilateral scrotal pain.|They report nausea and vomiting.|They have no dysuria.|The affected testis is high-riding with a transverse lie.|The ipsilateral cremasteric reflex is absent.|Doppler demonstrates markedly reduced blood flow on the painful side.","A twisted spermatic cord compromises testicular perfusion and explains the abrupt pain and examination findings."],
["gu-w16-03","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","BPH;benign prostatic enlargement","An older adult reports progressively slower urination.|They also report nocturia and hesitancy.|The stream is intermittent and feels incomplete afterward.|Urinalysis shows no evidence of infection.|The post-void residual is elevated.|Rectal examination shows a smooth symmetrically enlarged prostate.","A gradually progressive outlet obstruction with smooth prostatic enlargement is consistent with benign prostatic hyperplasia."],
["gu-w17-02","Ureteral stone","ureterolithiasis;nephrolithiasis;kidney stone;renal colic","An adult develops severe flank pain in waves.|They cannot find a comfortable position.|The pain tracks toward the groin.|Urinalysis shows microscopic hematuria.|Ultrasound shows mild hydronephrosis on the symptomatic side.|Noncontrast CT identifies a calculus in the distal ureter.","The distal ureteral calculus accounts for the colicky pain, hematuria, and upstream dilation."],
["b2-w6-03","Lynch syndrome","hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer;HNPCC","An adult develops colon cancer before age 50.|The tumour is in the proximal colon.|A first-degree relative had endometrial cancer at a young age.|There is no history of extensive colonic polyposis.|Tumour testing shows loss of MSH2 and MSH6 expression.|Germline testing identifies a pathogenic MSH2 variant.","A germline mismatch repair defect explains the early cancers and tumour protein loss."],
["b2-w6-02","Ductal carcinoma in situ","DCIS","A patient feels well and has no palpable breast mass.|Screening mammography detects clustered microcalcifications.|Diagnostic views show fine pleomorphic calcifications.|Stereotactic biopsy samples the abnormal area.|Atypical epithelial cells fill the ductal spaces.|The myoepithelial layer and basement membrane remain intact around the involved ducts.","The malignant epithelial proliferation remains confined within the ducts, which is characteristic of DCIS."],
["b4-w15-02","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","PJP;PCP;pneumocystis pneumonia","An immunocompromised patient has several weeks of worsening shortness of breath.|They have a dry cough and low-grade fever.|Oxygen saturation falls with walking.|Their CD4 count is very low.|CT shows bilateral diffuse ground-glass opacities.|Organisms consistent with Pneumocystis jirovecii are demonstrated in bronchoalveolar lavage.","The subacute dry cough, hypoxemia, and diffuse opacities fit Pneumocystis pneumonia in a patient with advanced cellular immunosuppression."],
["b4-w13-15","Tetanus","generalized tetanus","An adult develops increasing stiffness after gardening.|A deep puncture wound on their foot has started to heal.|Their vaccination history is incomplete.|They have difficulty opening their mouth.|Loud noises trigger painful generalized muscle spasms.|They remain alert throughout the spasms.","Trismus and stimulus-triggered spasms after a contaminated wound fit tetanus."],
["b4-w13-15","Botulism","foodborne botulism","Several people who shared a meal develop weakness.|The meal included home-preserved food.|They develop blurry vision and difficulty swallowing.|Pupils react sluggishly.|Weakness descends from the cranial muscles and sensation remains intact.|Botulinum toxin is detected in a serum sample.","Botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release and produces descending flaccid paralysis."],
["cardio-w4-07","Ventricular septal defect","VSD","An infant is referred for a new murmur.|The infant has no cyanosis.|Feeds take longer than expected.|There is a harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border.|Echocardiography shows a defect in the membranous septum.|Colour Doppler shows left-to-right flow between the ventricles.","Flow through a ventricular septal defect produces the characteristic lower sternal border murmur."],
["gi-w11-04","Colorectal adenocarcinoma","colon cancer;colorectal cancer;colon adenocarcinoma","An older adult presents with fatigue and weight loss.|Bloodwork shows iron deficiency anemia.|They report no visible rectal bleeding.|Colonoscopy identifies an ulcerated mass in the ascending colon.|Biopsy shows malignant gland formation.|Irregular glands invade the surrounding stroma.","The invasive gland-forming tumour is colorectal adenocarcinoma. Chronic occult bleeding can explain the iron deficiency."],
["gi-w12-09","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","pancreatic adenocarcinoma;pancreatic cancer;PDAC","An older adult develops progressive fatigue and weight loss.|They have pale stools and dark urine.|The jaundice has developed with little abdominal pain.|The liver panel shows direct hyperbilirubinemia and a cholestatic pattern.|CT shows a pancreatic head mass with upstream bile and pancreatic duct dilation.|Biopsy shows malignant duct-forming glands in desmoplastic stroma.","A pancreatic head tumour can obstruct the bile duct. The biopsy supports pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma."],
["gu-w16-07","Urothelial carcinoma of the bladder","bladder cancer;bladder urothelial carcinoma;transitional cell carcinoma","An older adult notices blood in the urine.|The bleeding is painless and intermittent.|They have a substantial smoking history.|Urine culture is negative.|Cystoscopy shows a papillary lesion in the bladder.|Biopsy shows malignant urothelium.","Painless hematuria prompts evaluation of the urinary tract. The cystoscopic biopsy identifies the bladder tumour."],
["gu-w16-04","Prostate adenocarcinoma","prostate cancer;prostatic adenocarcinoma","An older adult is referred after an abnormal blood test.|They report minimal urinary symptoms.|Repeat PSA remains elevated.|Digital rectal examination finds a firm asymmetric nodule.|MRI identifies a suspicious peripheral-zone lesion.|Targeted biopsy shows infiltrating small malignant glands.","The peripheral-zone lesion and malignant glandular histology establish prostate adenocarcinoma."],
["endo-w1-02","Type 1 diabetes mellitus","T1DM","A teenager has several weeks of fatigue.|They are drinking and urinating frequently.|They have lost weight despite eating normally.|Random glucose is 19 mmol/L with classic symptoms.|C-peptide is low despite hyperglycemia.|GAD65 autoantibodies are detected.","Islet autoimmunity and reduced endogenous insulin secretion support type 1 diabetes."],
["endo-w1-05","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","T2DM","An adult reports gradually worsening thirst.|Several relatives have diabetes.|Examination shows acanthosis nigricans.|There is no ketosis at presentation.|A1c is 7.8% on repeat testing.|C-peptide is preserved and islet antibody testing is negative.","Repeated A1c in the diabetes range establishes diabetes. Clinical insulin resistance and preserved secretion favour type 2."],
["endo-w1-06","Diabetic ketoacidosis","DKA","A patient with diabetes presents with vomiting.|They missed several doses of insulin.|They are dehydrated and have abdominal pain.|Breathing is deep and rapid.|Bloodwork shows elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis.|Serum beta-hydroxybutyrate is markedly elevated.","Ketonaemia and metabolic acidosis in a patient with diabetes establish DKA."],
["endo-w1-06","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state","HHS","An older adult becomes progressively confused.|They have had several days of polyuria.|Examination shows marked dehydration.|Glucose is 48 mmol/L.|Effective osmolality is above 320 mOsm/kg.|There is no substantial ketonaemia or acidosis.","Severe hyperglycemia and hyperosmolality with minimal ketosis favour HHS."],
["endo-w1-11","Gestational diabetes mellitus","GDM;gestational diabetes","A pregnant patient feels well at a routine visit.|Glucose testing early in pregnancy was normal.|The pregnancy is now at 27 weeks.|A glucose screening challenge is abnormal.|A diagnostic oral glucose tolerance test follows.|The result meets pregnancy-specific criteria without meeting criteria for overt diabetes.","Hyperglycemia first diagnosed in this pregnancy at the usual screening interval fits gestational diabetes."],
["endo-w2-03","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Hashimoto disease;autoimmune thyroiditis","An adult develops fatigue and constipation.|They are more sensitive to cold.|Examination shows a firm painless goitre.|TSH is elevated.|Free T4 is low.|Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies are elevated.","The hormone pattern establishes primary hypothyroidism, and the goitre and antibodies support autoimmune thyroiditis."],
["endo-w2-03","Toxic multinodular goitre","toxic multinodular goiter;TMNG","An older adult develops weight loss and palpitations.|They have had an irregular goitre for years.|There is no thyroid tenderness.|TSH is suppressed and free T4 is high.|There is no orbitopathy.|Radionuclide imaging shows patchy areas of increased and decreased uptake.","Heterogeneous uptake in a nodular gland points to multiple autonomously functioning nodules."],
["endo-w2-03","Toxic thyroid adenoma","toxic adenoma;autonomous thyroid nodule","An adult reports heat intolerance and tremor.|There is a single palpable thyroid nodule.|TSH is suppressed.|Free T3 is elevated.|Thyrotropin receptor antibodies are negative.|Radionuclide imaging shows one hot nodule with suppression of the surrounding gland.","A single autonomously functioning nodule explains the focal uptake and suppression elsewhere."],
["endo-w2-04","Primary hyperparathyroidism","PHPT","Routine bloodwork shows persistent hypercalcemia.|The patient has had two kidney stones.|Kidney function is preserved.|Serum phosphate is low.|PTH is elevated despite hypercalcemia.|Urinary calcium excretion is not low.","Inappropriately elevated PTH in the presence of hypercalcemia indicates a PTH-dependent process consistent with primary hyperparathyroidism."],
["endo-w2-04","Postsurgical hypoparathyroidism","postoperative hypoparathyroidism;hypoparathyroidism","A patient develops tingling around the mouth.|They also have painful hand cramps.|They recently underwent total thyroidectomy.|Serum calcium is low.|Serum phosphate is elevated.|PTH is inappropriately low.","Reduced PTH after neck surgery explains the hypocalcemia, phosphate retention, and neuromuscular irritability."],
["endo-w2-05","Papillary thyroid carcinoma","papillary thyroid cancer;PTC","An adult notices a painless neck lump.|They have no symptoms of thyroid hormone excess.|Ultrasound shows a solid hypoechoic thyroid nodule.|The nodule contains punctate echogenic foci.|Sampling shows nuclear grooves and intranuclear inclusions.|The tumour shows papillary architecture and optically clear nuclei.","These characteristic nuclear features support papillary thyroid carcinoma."],
["endo-w2-05","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","medullary thyroid cancer;MTC","A thyroid nodule is found on examination.|A relative had thyroid cancer and an adrenal tumour.|TSH is normal.|Serum calcitonin is elevated.|Histology shows nests of tumour cells with stromal amyloid.|Tumour cells stain for calcitonin.","Calcitonin production identifies a thyroid C-cell tumour. The family history suggests a possible hereditary syndrome."],
["endo-w2-06","Osteoporosis","osteoporotic fragility fracture","An older adult has acute back pain after bending.|They have gradually lost height.|Imaging shows a vertebral compression fracture.|Calcium and phosphate are normal.|There is no destructive lesion at the fracture site.|DXA shows a femoral neck T-score of −2.8.","A low-trauma vertebral fracture and reduced bone mineral density are characteristic of osteoporosis."],
["endo-w2-07","Osteomalacia","vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia","An adult develops diffuse bone pain.|Rising from a chair has become difficult.|Vitamin D intake and sun exposure have been limited.|Alkaline phosphatase is elevated.|25-hydroxyvitamin D is markedly reduced.|Radiographs show transverse pseudofractures.","Impaired mineralization accounts for the bone pain, proximal weakness, and pseudofractures."],
["endo-w3-01","Primary aldosteronism","primary hyperaldosteronism;Conn syndrome","An adult has hypertension despite three medications.|They develop intermittent muscle weakness.|Potassium is low without diuretic use.|Bloodwork shows metabolic alkalosis.|Renin is suppressed.|Aldosterone remains inappropriately elevated on confirmatory testing.","Autonomous aldosterone secretion accounts for low renin, hypertension, and renal potassium loss."],
["endo-w3-01","Pheochromocytoma","pheo;phaeochromocytoma","An adult has spells of severe headache.|They also have sweating and pounding palpitations.|Blood pressure rises sharply during episodes.|The episodes resolve between attacks.|Plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated.|Imaging identifies an adrenal medullary mass.","Catecholamine excess and the adrenal lesion point to pheochromocytoma."],
["endo-w3-01","Cushing disease","pituitary Cushing disease;ACTH secreting pituitary adenoma","An adult gains weight and develops hypertension.|They have proximal muscle weakness and wide violaceous striae.|Overnight dexamethasone fails to suppress cortisol.|Late-night salivary cortisol is repeatedly elevated.|ACTH is elevated.|Inferior petrosal sinus sampling identifies a pituitary ACTH source.","Endogenous cortisol excess driven by pituitary ACTH is Cushing disease."],
["endo-w3-02","Prolactinoma","prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma","A patient has several months of amenorrhea.|They also have spontaneous milky nipple discharge.|Pregnancy testing is negative.|Thyroid function is normal and there is no dopamine-blocking medication exposure.|Prolactin is markedly elevated.|MRI shows a pituitary adenoma.","Marked prolactin elevation with a pituitary lesion and compatible symptoms supports a prolactinoma."],
["endo-w3-02","Acromegaly","growth hormone secreting adenoma","An adult notices that rings and shoes no longer fit.|They have developed headaches and excessive sweating.|Examination shows a prominent jaw and enlarged hands.|Age-adjusted IGF-1 is elevated.|Growth hormone fails to suppress after oral glucose.|MRI shows a pituitary adenoma.","Growth hormone excess after epiphyseal closure produces acral and facial enlargement."],
["endo-w3-02","Central diabetes insipidus","arginine vasopressin deficiency;central DI;AVP-D","A patient becomes extremely thirsty after pituitary surgery.|Urine output rises sharply.|Serum sodium increases.|Urine remains dilute despite high plasma osmolality.|Blood glucose is normal.|Urine osmolality rises substantially after desmopressin.","A strong response to desmopressin supports deficient vasopressin secretion."],
["endo-w3-10","Polycystic ovary syndrome","PCOS;polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome;PMOS","A patient reports irregular periods since late adolescence.|They also have progressive facial hair growth.|Pregnancy testing is negative.|Androgen levels are mildly elevated.|TSH, prolactin, and 17-hydroxyprogesterone are unremarkable.|There is persistent ovulatory dysfunction with no rapid virilization.","Ovulatory dysfunction and androgen excess support PCOS after alternative causes are excluded."],
["repro-w4-05","Bacterial vaginosis","BV","A patient notices a change in vaginal discharge.|There is little itching or pain.|The discharge is thin and homogeneous.|Vaginal pH is elevated.|Adding potassium hydroxide produces an amine odour.|Microscopy shows clue cells.","The discharge, elevated pH, amine odour, and clue cells fit bacterial vaginosis."],
["repro-w4-05","Vulvovaginal candidiasis","vaginal candidiasis;yeast infection","A patient reports intense vulvar itching.|They recently took antibiotics.|Examination shows vulvar erythema.|There is thick white discharge.|Vaginal pH is within the usual acidic range.|Microscopy shows budding yeast and pseudohyphae.","Pruritus and yeast forms on microscopy support vulvovaginal candidiasis."],
["repro-w4-05","Trichomoniasis","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","A patient presents with abnormal discharge and irritation.|They have discomfort during urination.|The discharge is yellow-green.|Vaginal pH is elevated.|The cervix has punctate erythema.|A nucleic acid test detects Trichomonas vaginalis.","The positive nucleic acid test identifies the cause of the vaginitis."],
["repro-w4-08","Pelvic inflammatory disease","PID","A patient develops bilateral lower abdominal pain.|They report pain during intercourse.|They have new mucopurulent discharge.|Pregnancy testing is negative.|Pelvic examination elicits cervical motion and bilateral adnexal tenderness.|A cervical test is positive for Chlamydia trachomatis.","Pelvic tenderness and cervical inflammation support ascending infection in this vignette."],
["repro-w4-09","Tubal ectopic pregnancy","ectopic pregnancy","A patient develops unilateral lower abdominal pain.|They report light vaginal bleeding.|They recently missed a period.|Pregnancy testing is positive.|Transvaginal ultrasound shows no intrauterine gestational sac.|A yolk sac is visible within a gestational sac in the fallopian tube.","An extrauterine gestational sac containing a yolk sac establishes a tubal ectopic pregnancy."],
["repro-w4-09","Incomplete miscarriage","incomplete abortion;incomplete spontaneous abortion","A patient in early pregnancy develops cramping and heavy bleeding.|They have passed some tissue.|Bleeding and cramps continue afterward.|The cervix is open.|A prior scan confirmed an intrauterine pregnancy.|Ultrasound shows retained intrauterine products of conception.","Partial passage with retained products supports an incomplete miscarriage."],
["repro-w4-09","Missed miscarriage","missed abortion;missed pregnancy loss","A patient in early pregnancy has mild spotting.|There has been no tissue passage.|The cervix is closed.|Ultrasound identifies an intrauterine embryo.|The crown–rump length is 9 mm.|Transvaginal ultrasound shows no embryonic cardiac activity.","An embryo of this measured size without cardiac activity establishes pregnancy loss. The pregnancy tissue remains in the uterus."],
["repro-w4-10","Endometriosis","deep infiltrating endometriosis","A patient has progressively painful periods.|They also report deep pain with intercourse.|Bowel movements are painful around menstruation.|Pregnancy has not occurred despite trying.|Examination shows posterior pelvic tenderness.|Laparoscopy identifies implants containing endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus.","Ectopic endometrial tissue explains the cyclical symptoms and may contribute to infertility."],
["repro-w4-11","Ovarian torsion","adnexal torsion","A patient develops abrupt severe unilateral pelvic pain.|They have nausea and vomiting.|Pregnancy testing is negative.|Ultrasound shows an enlarged ovary with peripheral follicles.|A twisted vascular pedicle is visible.|Laparoscopy confirms rotation of the ovary around its pedicle.","Twisting compromises adnexal blood flow and explains the acute unilateral pain."],
["repro-w4-13","Adenomyosis","uterine adenomyosis","A patient reports worsening dysmenorrhea.|Periods have become heavier.|Examination finds a diffusely enlarged tender uterus.|Ultrasound shows heterogeneous myometrium.|There are small myometrial cysts.|MRI shows thickening of the uterine junctional zone.","Endometrial tissue within the myometrium can produce a diffusely enlarged uterus and painful heavy bleeding."],
["repro-w4-15","Uterine leiomyoma","fibroid;uterine fibroids;leiomyoma","A patient presents with heavy cyclic bleeding.|They also notice pelvic pressure.|The uterus feels irregularly enlarged.|Ultrasound shows a well-circumscribed solid uterine lesion.|The lesion projects into the uterine cavity.|Histology shows whorled bundles of benign smooth muscle.","A submucosal leiomyoma distorts the uterine cavity and can cause heavy menstrual bleeding."],
["repro-w5-04","Mature cystic teratoma","ovarian dermoid;dermoid cyst","An adnexal mass is found incidentally.|The patient has no constitutional symptoms.|Ultrasound shows a complex ovarian lesion.|Imaging demonstrates macroscopic fat and calcification.|Surgery reveals a cyst containing hair and sebaceous material.|Histology shows mature tissues from more than one germ layer.","Fat, hair, and other mature tissue elements identify a mature cystic teratoma."],
["repro-w5-05","Endometrial carcinoma","endometrial cancer","A postmenopausal patient reports new vaginal bleeding.|They are not taking hormone therapy.|The cervix appears normal.|Transvaginal ultrasound shows a thickened endometrium.|Endometrial sampling reveals crowded atypical glands.|Histology confirms malignant endometrial epithelium.","Postmenopausal bleeding warrants evaluation, and the endometrial biopsy establishes malignancy."],
["repro-w5-09","Complete hydatidiform mole","complete molar pregnancy;complete mole","A patient in early pregnancy develops bleeding and severe vomiting.|The uterus is larger than expected for dates.|Serum hCG is markedly elevated.|Ultrasound shows diffuse cystic placental tissue without a fetus.|Histology shows diffuse villous swelling and circumferential trophoblast proliferation.|Villous cytotrophoblast and stromal cells lack p57 expression.","The ultrasound and histology support a complete mole. Absent p57 expression fits the usual androgenetic origin."],
["repro-w5-12","Preeclampsia","pre-eclampsia","A patient at 32 weeks develops new hypertension.|They were normotensive before 20 weeks.|Repeat measurements remain elevated.|They have a persistent headache.|Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is elevated.|There is no pre-existing kidney disease to explain the new proteinuria.","New hypertension after 20 weeks with new proteinuria supports preeclampsia."],
["repro-w5-12","HELLP syndrome","HELLP","A patient at 34 weeks has nausea and right upper quadrant pain.|Blood pressure is elevated.|Platelets fall below 100 × 10⁹/L.|AST and ALT are elevated.|LDH rises and haptoglobin is low.|The smear shows red cell fragmentation.","Hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets define HELLP syndrome."],
["repro-w6-01","Placenta previa","placenta praevia","A patient at 33 weeks develops vaginal bleeding.|The bleeding is painless.|The uterus is soft between assessments.|There is no sustained uterine tenderness.|The fetus has a reassuring tracing.|Transvaginal ultrasound shows placental tissue covering the internal cervical os.","A placenta covering the internal os explains the painless antepartum bleeding."],
["repro-w6-01","Placental abruption","abruptio placentae;abruption","A patient late in pregnancy develops abrupt abdominal pain.|There is vaginal bleeding.|The uterus is tender and firm.|Contractions occur frequently without full relaxation.|The fetal tracing deteriorates.|After delivery, a retroplacental clot is identified.","Pain, uterine hypertonicity, and a retroplacental clot support premature placental separation."],
["repro-w6-01","Vasa previa","vasa praevia","A patient develops vaginal bleeding immediately after membrane rupture.|Their blood pressure remains stable.|The fetal heart rate falls abruptly.|The visible blood loss seems small relative to the fetal compromise.|Placental examination shows a velamentous cord insertion.|A torn unprotected fetal vessel crosses the internal cervical os.","Rupture of an exposed fetal vessel explains hemorrhage with sudden fetal compromise and relatively stable maternal vital signs."],
["repro-w6-03","Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes","PPROM;preterm premature rupture of membranes","A patient at 31 weeks notices a sudden gush of fluid.|There is continued leakage afterward.|They do not report regular contractions.|Sterile speculum examination shows clear fluid pooling.|Microscopy shows ferning.|The membrane rupture occurred before the onset of labour.","Prelabour membrane rupture before 37 weeks is PPROM."],
["repro-w6-06","Uterine atony","atonic postpartum hemorrhage;atonic postpartum haemorrhage","Heavy vaginal bleeding begins shortly after delivery.|The placenta appears complete.|No major genital tract tear is identified.|The fundus is enlarged.|On palpation the uterus is soft and boggy.|Bleeding decreases when the uterus contracts.","Inadequate uterine contraction leaves placental bed vessels open, producing atonic postpartum hemorrhage."],
["repro-w6-08","Postpartum endometritis","puerperal endometritis","A patient develops fever two days after delivery.|Delivery was by cesarean after prolonged labour.|They report increasing lower abdominal pain.|The uterine fundus is tender.|Lochia has a foul odour.|The incision is clean and there is no focal breast redness.","Fever and uterine tenderness with malodorous lochia support infection of the postpartum endometrium."],
["msk-w7-02.2","Lumbar disc herniation with L5 radiculopathy","L5 radiculopathy;lumbar disc herniation;sciatica","An adult develops back pain after lifting.|Pain radiates down the lateral leg.|Coughing worsens the pain.|Great toe extension is weak.|Straight-leg raise reproduces the leg pain.|MRI shows an L4–L5 paracentral disc extrusion impinging on the traversing root.","An L4–L5 paracentral extrusion typically compresses the traversing L5 root, matching the dorsiflexion and great toe weakness."],
["msk-w7-02.2","Lumbar spinal stenosis","lumbar canal stenosis;neurogenic claudication","An older adult develops aching in both legs when walking.|Standing for several minutes can also trigger symptoms.|Sitting relieves the pain.|They can cycle farther than they can walk.|Foot pulses are palpable.|MRI shows marked lumbar central canal narrowing.","Relief with flexion and provocation by standing suggest neurogenic claudication from spinal stenosis."],
["msk-w7-03.2","Cauda equina syndrome","CES","An adult with new back pain develops bilateral leg symptoms.|They notice numbness between the legs.|They cannot initiate urination.|Post-void residual is markedly elevated.|Examination shows saddle sensory loss.|MRI shows a large central disc extrusion compressing multiple lumbar roots.","Bladder dysfunction and saddle anesthesia with compression of the cauda equina establish the syndrome."],
["msk-w8-02.2","Supraspinatus tendon tear","supraspinatus tear;rotator cuff tear","An adult develops shoulder pain following a fall.|Active elevation is weak and painful.|Passive range of motion is near normal.|Resisted abduction in the scapular plane is weak.|They cannot smoothly lower the arm from abduction.|Ultrasound shows a full-thickness tendon defect at the superior greater tuberosity.","Weakness with preserved passive motion and a tendon defect at the supraspinatus insertion identify the tear."],
["msk-w8-02.2","Adhesive capsulitis","frozen shoulder","A patient with diabetes develops gradual shoulder pain.|They recall no injury.|Reaching behind the back becomes difficult.|Both active and passive motion are restricted.|Passive external rotation is particularly limited.|Radiographs show no advanced glenohumeral joint degeneration.","Global restriction of passive and active motion, especially external rotation, supports adhesive capsulitis."],
["msk-w8-05.2","Lateral epicondylalgia","tennis elbow;lateral epicondylitis","An adult develops lateral elbow pain after repetitive gripping.|There was no single traumatic event.|The lateral epicondyle is tender.|Resisted wrist extension reproduces the pain.|Passive elbow motion is preserved.|There is no paresthesia or objective motor deficit.","Pain at the common extensor origin with resisted wrist extension is characteristic of lateral epicondylalgia."],
["msk-w8-07.2","Cubital tunnel syndrome","ulnar neuropathy at the elbow;ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow","A patient reports tingling in the little and ring fingers.|Symptoms worsen with prolonged elbow flexion.|There is weakness of finger abduction.|Tapping behind the medial epicondyle reproduces symptoms.|Sensation over the dorsal ulnar hand is reduced.|Nerve conduction studies show slowing across the elbow.","The sensory distribution and focal slowing localize the ulnar neuropathy to the elbow."],
["msk-w8-07.2","Carpal tunnel syndrome","CTS;median neuropathy at the wrist","A patient wakes with hand tingling.|Shaking the hand temporarily helps.|Symptoms affect the thumb, index, and middle fingers.|Sustained wrist flexion reproduces the symptoms.|Sensation over the thenar eminence is preserved.|Nerve conduction shows delayed median sensory conduction across the wrist.","Median nerve compression at the wrist accounts for the digit symptoms and sparing of the thenar skin."],
["msk-w9-02","Scaphoid fracture","fractured scaphoid","An adult falls onto an outstretched hand.|The radial side of the wrist is painful.|There is tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox.|Axial thumb loading reproduces pain.|Initial radiographs are unrevealing.|MRI shows a fracture through the waist of a carpal bone on the radial side.","A scaphoid waist fracture may be occult on initial radiographs."],
["msk-w9-05","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome","GTPS;greater trochanteric bursitis;trochanteric bursitis","An adult has pain on the outside of the hip.|Lying on that side worsens the pain.|Stair climbing is uncomfortable.|There is focal tenderness over the greater trochanter.|Resisted hip abduction reproduces symptoms.|Passive hip rotation produces no significant groin pain.","Focal lateral pain and loading of the hip abductors support greater trochanteric pain syndrome."],
["msk-w9-06.2","Anterior cruciate ligament tear","ACL tear;ACL rupture","An athlete injures a knee while pivoting.|They felt a pop and could not continue playing.|The knee swelled within a few hours.|They describe giving way when turning.|Lachman testing shows increased anterior translation and a soft endpoint.|MRI shows discontinuity of the ligament that resists anterior tibial translation.","The mechanism, rapid effusion, and abnormal Lachman test support ACL rupture."],
["msk-w9-07.2","Medial meniscus tear","medial meniscal tear;meniscal tear;meniscus tear","An adult twists a loaded knee while crouching.|Swelling develops over the next day.|The knee catches with deep flexion.|There is focal medial joint line tenderness.|Rotating the flexed knee reproduces a painful click.|MRI shows a tear extending to the articular surface of the medial meniscus.","Joint line pain and mechanical catching after a loaded twist fit a meniscal tear."],
["msk-w10-01.2","Achilles tendon rupture","Achilles tear;Achilles rupture","An athlete feels as though someone kicked the back of their ankle.|They cannot push off normally.|There is bruising behind the ankle.|A gap is palpable above the calcaneus.|Single-leg heel rise is impossible.|Squeezing the calf produces no plantarflexion.","An absent plantarflexion response to calf squeeze and a tendon gap support Achilles rupture."],
["msk-w10-09","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","SCFE;slipped upper femoral epiphysis;SUFE","An adolescent develops a limp and intermittent knee pain.|The knee examination is unremarkable.|Hip internal rotation is limited.|The hip externally rotates during passive flexion.|Imaging shows widening of the proximal femoral physis.|The femoral head epiphysis is displaced posteroinferiorly relative to the neck.","The hip findings and displacement through the proximal femoral physis identify SCFE. Hip pathology can present as referred knee pain."],
["msk-w10-09","Legg–Calvé–Perthes disease","Perthes disease;Legg Calve Perthes","A seven-year-old develops a progressive limp.|There is intermittent hip and thigh pain.|The child is afebrile and otherwise well.|Hip abduction and internal rotation are limited.|Imaging shows increased density of the femoral head epiphysis.|Follow-up imaging shows fragmentation and flattening of the femoral head.","Osteonecrosis of the developing femoral head causes the characteristic imaging progression."],
["msk-w10-11","Osteosarcoma","osteogenic sarcoma","A teenager reports progressive pain above the knee.|There is firm swelling over the distal thigh.|Pain persists at rest.|Imaging shows an aggressive metaphyseal lesion.|There is a spiculated periosteal reaction.|Biopsy shows malignant cells producing osteoid.","Malignant osteoid on biopsy is diagnostic of osteosarcoma."],
["msk-w11-01","Osteomyelitis","acute hematogenous osteomyelitis","A child develops fever and focal shin pain.|They refuse to bear weight.|There is point tenderness over the tibial metaphysis.|CRP is elevated.|MRI shows metaphyseal marrow edema and a subperiosteal collection.|Culture from the collection grows Staphylococcus aureus.","The focal bone findings and positive culture support bacterial osteomyelitis."],
["msk-w11-08","Septic arthritis","infectious arthritis;bacterial arthritis","An older adult develops an acutely swollen knee.|They have fever and severe pain with passive motion.|Effusion is confirmed on examination.|Aspirate contains many neutrophils.|Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci.|Synovial fluid culture grows Staphylococcus aureus.","A pathogen cultured from synovial fluid establishes septic arthritis in this presentation."],
["msk-w11-02","Rheumatoid arthritis","RA","An adult develops gradually worsening hand pain.|Morning stiffness lasts more than an hour.|Swelling affects both wrists and multiple MCP and PIP joints.|The DIP joints are relatively spared.|Anti-CCP antibodies are positive.|Hand radiographs show marginal erosions.","Symmetric inflammatory small-joint synovitis with anti-CCP and marginal erosions supports RA."],
["msk-w11-03","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","systemic JIA;sJIA;Still disease","A child has recurring high fevers for three weeks.|They appear much better between spikes.|A transient salmon-coloured rash appears with fever.|Several joints have persistent objective swelling.|Ferritin and inflammatory markers are elevated.|Evaluation finds no infectious or malignant explanation.","Quotidian fever, evanescent rash, and persistent arthritis support systemic JIA after excluding major alternatives."],
["msk-w11-03","Enthesitis-related arthritis","ERA;enthesitis related JIA","An adolescent develops a swollen knee and heel pain.|Examination shows Achilles insertion tenderness.|Morning stiffness improves with movement.|There is intermittent buttock pain.|HLA-B27 is positive.|MRI shows active sacroiliitis.","Arthritis plus enthesitis in an adolescent supports the enthesitis-related form of JIA."],
["msk-w11-05","Non-radiographic axial spondyloarthritis","nr-axSpA;nonradiographic axial spondyloarthritis","A young adult has several months of low back pain.|Pain improves with activity and wakes them late in the night.|They have alternating buttock pain.|A previous episode of anterior uveitis is documented.|Sacroiliac radiographs show no definite structural sacroiliitis.|MRI shows active sacroiliac inflammation in a typical distribution.","The inflammatory presentation and MRI support axial spondyloarthritis. The absence of definite radiographic sacroiliitis fits the non-radiographic form."],
["msk-w11-06","Systemic lupus erythematosus","SLE;lupus","A young adult develops fatigue and symmetric hand arthritis.|They have painless oral ulcers.|A rash over the cheeks worsens with sun exposure.|Urinalysis shows proteinuria and red cell casts.|Complement levels are low.|Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are positive.","Inflammatory arthritis, mucocutaneous disease, and glomerular findings with supportive serology point to SLE."],
["msk-w11-06","Sjögren disease","Sjögren syndrome;Sjögren's syndrome;Sjögren's disease","An adult has gritty eyes and intermittent joint pain.|They sip water to swallow dry foods.|Dental caries have increased.|Schirmer testing shows reduced tear production.|Unstimulated salivary flow is low.|Anti-SSA antibodies are detected.","Objective lacrimal and salivary dysfunction with anti-SSA supports Sjögren disease."],
["msk-w11-06","Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis","limited systemic sclerosis;systemic sclerosis;scleroderma;CREST","An adult reports years of cold-induced colour changes in the fingers.|They develop reflux and progressive skin tightening.|Examination shows telangiectasias and sclerodactyly.|Nailfold capillaries are abnormal.|Skin thickening is confined to distal extremities and the face.|Anticentromere antibodies are detected.","The distal skin distribution, Raynaud phenomenon, and anticentromere antibodies support limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis."],
["msk-w11-06","Dermatomyositis","DM myositis","An adult has difficulty climbing stairs and lifting objects overhead.|Examination shows symmetric proximal weakness.|Creatine kinase is elevated.|There are violaceous eyelids.|Scaly papules over the MCP and PIP joints are visible.|Muscle biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy.","Proximal myopathy with a heliotrope rash and Gottron papules supports dermatomyositis."],
["msk-w11-07","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","GPA;Wegener granulomatosis","An adult has persistent sinus symptoms and recurrent nosebleeds.|They develop cough and hemoptysis.|Chest imaging shows cavitating nodules.|Urine microscopy shows red cell casts.|PR3-ANCA is positive.|A respiratory biopsy shows necrotizing granulomatous inflammation.","Upper and lower airway involvement with glomerulonephritis, PR3-ANCA, and granulomatous inflammation supports GPA."],
["msk-w11-07","IgA vasculitis","Henoch Schonlein purpura;HSP","A child develops ankle pain after an upper respiratory illness.|They have intermittent abdominal pain.|A nonblanching palpable rash appears on the legs and buttocks.|The platelet count is normal.|Urinalysis shows microscopic hematuria.|Skin biopsy shows small-vessel vasculitis with IgA deposition.","Palpable purpura with abdominal, joint, and renal involvement fits IgA vasculitis."],
["msk-w12-03","Fibromyalgia","fibromyalgia syndrome","An adult has widespread pain for over six months.|They wake feeling unrefreshed.|They report fatigue and trouble concentrating.|Examination shows diffuse tenderness without joint swelling.|Strength is preserved and inflammatory markers are unremarkable.|Symptoms span multiple body regions and persist across visits.","Widespread pain with fatigue, sleep disturbance, and cognitive symptoms supports fibromyalgia."],
].map(([note,answer,aliases,clues,explanation])=>({note,answer,aliases:aliases.split(';').filter(Boolean),clues:clues.split('|'),explanation}));
